The Kashmir Centre has submitted a formal dossier to UK Home Secretary Shabana Mahmood, urging an urgent assessment of RSS chief Mohan Bhagwat under the "non-conducive to the public good" provisions of UK Immigration Rules ahead of his planned London visit, part of a three-country centenary tour covering the US, Canada, and the UK. It has separately referred Bhagwat to the Metropolitan Police and the National Police Chiefs' Council to assess whether his London events could engage religious-hatred offences under the Public Order Act 1986.

Executive Director Dr Muzzammil Ayyub Thakur cited the RSS's history of being banned in India in 1948, 1975, and 1992, arguing the UK should apply the same public-good standard it used to bar commentators Valentina Gomez, Cenk Uygur, and Hasan Piker earlier this year. Bhagwat landed in New York on August 25, where his tour has already drawn objections from Mayor Zohran Mamdani and the US Commission on International Religious Freedom.
